Let the impulse be applied perpendicular to the rod at end B.

- Mass of rod, \(m = 2\,\text{kg}\)
- Length, \(L = 30\,\text{cm} = 0.3\,\text{m}\)
- Impulse, \(J = 0.2\,\text{Ns}\)

Moment of inertia of the rod about its centre is  
\[
I=\frac{mL^2}{12}
\]

The impulse at end B produces angular impulse about the centre:  
\[
L_{\text{ang}}=J\cdot \frac{L}{2}
\]

So angular velocity is  
\[
\omega=\frac{L_{\text{ang}}}{I}
=\frac{J(L/2)}{mL^2/12}
=\frac{6J}{mL}
\]

Now,  
\[
\omega=\frac{6\times0.2}{2\times0.3}
=\frac{1.2}{0.6}
=2\,\text{rad/s}
\]

Time to rotate through a right angle, i.e. \(\theta=\frac{\pi}{2}\) rad:  
\[
t=\frac{\theta}{\omega}
=\frac{\pi/2}{2}
=\frac{\pi}{4}\,\text{s}
\]

Comparing with \(\frac{\pi}{x}\),  
\[
x=4
\]

